This error, often abbreviated UMR (Uninitialised Memory Read), means the program attempted to read from memory that was allocated but never explicitly written to (initialized). Example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
int main() {
    int *x = malloc(sizeof(int));
    // *x is uninitialized here
    int y = *x; // UMR: Use of uninitialised value
    printf("%d\n", y);
    free(x);
    return 0;
}
```

These errors indicate an attempt to access memory outside the bounds of an allocated block. "Invalid read" means reading out of bounds, while "Invalid write" means writing out of bounds. A common cause is off-by-one errors in array indexing or pointer arithmetic, leading to accessing memory just before or after the intended block.
Example of invalid write:
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
int main() {
    int *arr = malloc(5 * sizeof(int));
    arr[5] = 10; // Invalid write (index 5 is out of bounds for 0-4)
    free(arr);
    return 0;
}
```
